 (Lewis base, lone pair) binds Ag⁺ (Lewis acid): Ag⁺ + 2NH₃ ⇌ Ag(NH₃)₂⁺, Kf 1.7 × 10⁷ (OpenStax 15.2). Free Ag⁺ falls far below what AgCl's Ksp allows, so AgCl keeps dissolving (coupled equilibria). With ammonia in excess, free [Ag⁺] = Kd [complex] ÷ [NH₃]², millions of times less than the silver present.", "target": "A ligand that binds a metal ion strongly leaves almost no free metal ion; with the ligand in excess, x = Kd C ÷ Lⁿ.", "keywords": ["complex ion", "ligand", "lone pair", "Lewis acid", "Lewis base", "ammonia", "silver ion", "Kf", "binds", "free silver"], "liquids": ["DP1", "DP2", "DP3", "DP4", "DP5", "DP6"], "kinds": ["free_metal"], "fits": ["DP1", "DP2", "DP3", "DP4", "DP5", "DP6"], "media": {"image_with_question": "ch15/Disappearing_precipitate.jpg", "image_after_account": "", "image_alt": "A lab bench: a bottle labeled conc. NH3 beside a beaker of clear liquid with an ion probe, and a meter reading 1.2E-9 M.", "simulation": "ch15/complex_simulator.html?complex={complex}&C={C}&L={L}&theme={theme}", "simulation_opens": "meaning", "credit": "AI image made by the instructor with Gemini"}, "label_words": ["complex ion", "ligand", "formation constant", "Lewis acid", "Lewis base", "coupled equilibria"], "role": "worked", "items": {"add": [], "remove": []}}, {"title": "The Darkroom Fixer", "question": "Black-and-white film is developed, then soaked in a fixer bath of sodium thiosulfate, which dissolves the leftover silver bromide so the picture stops darkening in light. A silver-ion electrode in used fixer reads almost no free silver ion. Which species holds the silver, and why is so little free silver left?", "model_answer": "S₂O₃²⁻ binds Ag⁺: Ag⁺ + 2S₂O₃²⁻ ⇌ Ag(S₂O₃)₂³⁻, Kf 4.7 × 10¹³ (OpenStax 15.3, Example 15.16). With thiosulfate in excess, nearly all silver is complexed; free [Ag⁺] = Kd [complex] ÷ [S₂O₃²⁻]², about 10¹¹ to 10¹³ times less than the silver present.", "target": "With a very large Kf and the ligand in excess, the free metal ion is x = Kd C 