e pair, and a silver ion can accept one. What can two ammonia molecules and one silver ion form?", "park_text": "Each silver ion is held by two ammonia molecules as Ag(NH₃)₂⁺. So little silver stays free that silver chloride keeps dissolving.", "status": "approved (instructor, 17 Oct 2026)", "show": "Here is how a chemist would put it: ammonia's lone pairs bind silver ions as Ag(NH₃)₂⁺; so little silver stays free that silver chloride keeps dissolving. Now say it in your own words."}, "meaning_given": {"state": "meaning_given", "stage": "meaning", "label_when_done": "Meaning stated", "ask": "Why does adding ammonia make silver chloride, an insoluble salt, dissolve? Which two equilibria are linked?", "switch_ask": "If the free silver ion falls, what happens to Q for silver chloride?", "status": "approved (instructor, 17 Oct 2026)", "show": "The complex pulls free silver far below what silver chloride's Ksp allows, so Q for AgCl drops below Ksp and more solid dissolves: two coupled equilibria. Now answer in your own words."}, "relation_chosen": {"state": "relation_chosen", "status": "approved (instructor, 17 Oct 2026)", "show": "The complex forms almost completely; to find the free silver, use its breakup: \\( K_d = \\dfrac{1}{K_f} = \\dfrac{[\\mathrm{Ag^+}][\\mathrm{NH_3}]^2}{[\\mathrm{Ag(NH_3)_2^+}]} \\). Pick it."}, "complete_found@v:core": {"state": "complete_found", "status": "approved (instructor, 17 Oct 2026)", "show": "After mixing (100.0 mL): Ag⁺ 0.0050 M, NH₃ 0.500 M. All the silver forms the complex: [complex] = 0.0050 M; NH₃ left = 0.500 − 2 × 0.0050 = 0.490 M. Type both.", "version": "core"}, "complete_found@v:variant": {"state": "complete_found", "status": "approved (instructor, 17 Oct 2026)", "show": "After mixing (100.0 mL): Ag⁺ 0.0050 M, NH₃ 0.0101 M. [complex] = 0.0050 M; NH₃ left = 0.0101 − 2 × 0.0050 = 1.0 × 10⁻⁴ M. Type both.", "version": "variant"}, "Kd_found": {"state": "Kd_found", "status": "approved (instructor, 17 Oct 2026)", "show": "\\( K_d